syntax:
The basic syntax involves including the `<vector>`, `<list>`, `<deque>`, `<set>`, `<map>` headers, as needed, and then declaring and using the containers. For example:

```c++
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  std::vector<int> numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
  std::list<std::string> names;  // Empty list
  names.push_back("Alice");
  names.push_front("Bob");
  std::map<std::string, int> ages; //Map of string to int
  ages["Alice"] = 30;
  std::cout << ages["Alice"] << std::endl; // Accessing value using key 
  return 0;
}
```
**Best practices:** Choose the container that best suits your access patterns. Consider memory usage and performance implications of different container types. Use iterators for efficient traversal and manipulation of container elements. Avoid unnecessary copies by using references or pointers when appropriate.

code_example:
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
int main() {
  std::vector<int> numbers = {5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6};
  std::sort(numbers.begin(), numbers.end()); //Using STL algorithm for sorting
  for (int number : numbers) {
    std::cout << number << " ";
  }
  std::cout << std::endl;
  return 0;
}
